@@s001 Reading a Scala signature
@@s002 dato:placeholder A,ok:A replaced by Int,ora:parameters filled in
@@s003 The generic signature: <code>A</code> appears four times, and it is always the same type, still to be decided.
@@s004 The call chooses the type: <code>isSorted[Int](…)</code>.
@@s005 In the signature <b>every A becomes Int</b>, all together.
@@s006 The parameters are filled in order: <code>as</code> is the array, <code>ordered</code> is the function written on the fly.
@@s007 In the body, <code>ordered(as(0), as(1))</code> = (x, y) =&gt; x &lt;= y applied to (1, 2) = 1 &lt;= 2 = <b>true</b>.
@@s008 ora:piece you are reading,dato:already read,ok:filled by the call
@@s009 dato:what comes in,due:what comes out,ora:the function
@@s010 dato:first argument,ora:second argument,ok:result
@@s011 dato:first list (tree),ora:second list (function),ok:result Tree[String]
@@s012 dato:where A comes from,ora:the hidden parameter this,ok:result
@@s013 Reading a Scala signature
@@s014 The first line of a function says <b>what comes in</b> and <b>what comes out</b>. If you can read it in pieces, from left to right, half of the exercise is already done: often the types also tell you how to write the body.
@@s015 A signature, taken apart piece by piece
@@s016 Let us take the function of exercise 4 of week 1. It checks whether an array is sorted, according to a criterion that you pass it.
@@s017 isSorted, from left to right
@@s018 <code>def</code> = "I am defining a function". Like a method in Java.
@@s019 The name. You will use it to call it: <code>isSorted(…)</code>.
@@s020 <code>[A]</code> is a <b>placeholder for a type</b>. It is not a value: it says "here there will be a type, chosen by whoever calls". Every <code>A</code> after it is the same type.
@@s021 First parameter. It is written <b>name : type</b> (the opposite of Java). <code>as</code> is an array of elements of type <code>A</code>.
@@s022 Second parameter: <code>ordered</code>. Its type is <b>a function</b>: it takes two <code>A</code> and returns a <code>Boolean</code>.
@@s023 After the closing bracket, the <b>return type</b>. Here the function answers true or false.
@@s024 
@@s025 After <code>=</code> comes the body: an expression whose value is the result. No <code>return</code>.
@@s026 If you come from Java
@@s027 The same signature in Java would be <code>static &lt;A&gt; boolean isSorted(A[] as, BiPredicate&lt;A,A&gt; ordered)</code>. Scala puts the types <b>after</b> the names, uses <code>[A]</code> in place of <code>&lt;A&gt;</code> and writes the type of the function with an arrow instead of an interface.
@@s028 The six pieces of a signature, then the call
@@s029 When <code>[A]</code> is needed and when it is not
@@s030 Put <code>[A]</code> only when the function <b>does not need to know</b> what type of elements it has in hand. If it works with real numbers, the type is fixed.
@@s031 Without [A]
@@s032 <code>abs</code> compares with <code>0</code> and changes the sign: it really needs <code>Int</code>s.
@@s033 With [A]
@@s034 <code>isSorted</code> never compares the elements itself: it asks <code>ordered</code>. It works with numbers, strings, people.
@@s035 Who decides what <code>A</code> becomes? <b>Whoever calls</b>. Look at what happens in a real call:
@@s036 Call: A becomes Int
@@s037 I write the type in square brackets: <code>A</code> will be <code>Int</code>. (Often Scala understands it by itself and the square brackets can be omitted.)
@@s038 Inside the signature, every <code>A</code> is replaced by <code>Int</code>.
@@s039 The parameters are filled in order. <code>ordered</code> receives the function I wrote on the fly.
@@s040 When the body writes <code>ordered(a, b)</code>, it is calling the function received. The result is a <code>Boolean</code>, as the type promised.
@@s041 A becomes Int, everywhere
@@s042 The type of a function: the arrow <code>=></code>
@@s043 To the left of the arrow there is what comes in, to the right what comes out. That is all.
@@s044 Type
@@s045 Reads as
@@s046 A value of that type
@@s047 <code>Int => Boolean</code>
@@s048 takes an Int, gives a Boolean
@@s049 <code>x => x &lt; 0</code>
@@s050 <code>(Int, Int) => Int</code>
@@s051 takes two Ints, gives an Int
@@s052 <code>(A, A) => Boolean</code>
@@s053 takes two A, gives a Boolean
@@s054 <code>(x, y) => x &lt;= y</code> when A = Int
@@s055 takes an A, gives a B
@@s056 <code>_.toString</code> when A = Int and B = String
@@s057 takes nothing, gives an A
@@s058 <code>() => 42</code> (week 4)
@@s059 Rule
@@s060 <code>f: A => B</code> does not say <i>what</i> <code>f</code> does. It says only what <b>shape</b> it has: an A comes in, a B comes out. What it really does is decided by whoever passes the function at the moment of the call.
@@s061 What comes in, what comes out
@@s062 Arrows one inside the other
@@s063 In exercise 5 this signature appears, which looks like a Russian doll:
@@s064 Opening the doll, one shell at a time
@@s065 Several arrows in a row are read <b>from the right</b>: <code>X => Y => Z</code> means <code>X => (Y => Z)</code>. So the first thing that comes in is the array.
@@s066 Given the array, there comes out… another function. Not a Boolean, not yet.
@@s067 That function waits for the sorting criterion: <code>(A, A) => Boolean</code>. The round brackets are needed here: the criterion is <b>one</b> argument.
@@s068 Given the criterion too, the <code>Boolean</code> finally comes out.
@@s069 The call shows the structure: a pair of brackets for each "outer" arrow.
@@s070 A function that returns a function
@@s071 Several parameter lists
@@s072 A function can have several pairs of brackets. In the call you write the same number of them, in the same order.
@@s073 map on trees (week 3)
@@s074 Two type placeholders: <code>A</code> is what is in the leaves at the start, <code>B</code> what there will be afterwards.
@@s075 First list: the starting tree.
@@s076 Second list: the function that transforms each value. Alone, <code>A => B</code> does not say which transformation it is.
@@s077 In the call the two lists are filled in: <b>here</b> you decide that <code>f</code> is <code>_.toString</code>. So <code>B</code> = <code>String</code>.
@@s078 The result: a tree with the same shape, but of <code>B</code> (here strings).
@@s079 Watch out
@@s080 If the definition has two lists and you call with only one, <code>map(albero, f)</code>, it does not compile. Count the pairs of brackets in the definition.
@@s081 map(albero)(f): two pairs of brackets
@@s082 Methods inside an <code>enum</code>: where does A come from?
@@s083 From week 3 some functions are written <b>inside</b> the type, as methods. Then <code>A</code> is not among the square brackets of the method: it comes from the <code>enum</code>.
@@s084 map as a method of Option
@@s085 The <code>A</code> is born here, on the <code>enum</code>. The <code>+</code> is the variance (dedicated page in week 3).
@@s086 The method adds only <code>[B]</code>: <code>A</code> it already knows, because the method lives inside <code>Option[A]</code>.
@@s087 The parameter "the option I work on" is missing: it is <code>this</code>, the object on which you call the method. It is called with the dot: <code>Some(3).map(_ + 1)</code>.
@@s088 Some(3).map(_ + 1): A, B and this
